 ],
 "why": "The statement asks one universal question about all finite octal games. Each per-game periodicity proof (·45 through 4.406, 1956–2012) settles a single member of an infinite family, so I scored them instances, as I did the finite-range computations for Officers and ·007. All of them predate the snapshot except the 2026 OEIS extension for ·007. Every other post-snapshot paper treats a different class: infinite-code octal games, partizan octal games, Node-Kayles on trees, Inverse Treblecross, and subtraction or two-pile games. Each of these is scored variant. The GONC6 column only says there has been unspecified 'significant progress' on A2, so I scored it none. The close call was whether a single-game solution counts as a case. I chose instances because each settles one object, not a restriction to a class of games. Since all of those events predate the snapshot, the choice does not affect the result.",
 "doubts": "The A2 entry in GONC6, which claims 'significant progress', could not be read. If that progress is a class-level or general result, the problem may have moved during the window. The line 'easy to find in octal games' in Liang 2608.17654 is unresolved; if it means finite-code games, it would cut against the conjecture. I scored that paper knocked_down because the author withdrew the novelty claim. The per-game verification labels rest mostly on listings rather than primary papers.",
 "insufficient_evidence": false,
